  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Unseasonably mild through the week with a  \nchance of showers Wednesday afternoon into Friday.\n \nHigh pressure off the mid-Atlantic coast turns the flow toward\nthe southeast to south today providing warmer temperatures. As a\nfront approaches Wednesday the flow turns south to southwest so\nexpecting increasing clouds/dewpoints. Will see showers pushing\ninto the mountains by late afternoon. Some instability reaches\nthe far southwest VA counties, so not out of the question for\nsome thunderstorms heading into Wednesday evening. PoPs ramp up\nalong/ahead of the front Wednesday night, then get somewhat of a\nlull Thursday before another wave of low pressure pushes across\nThu night into Friday keeping higher pops in the Piedmont. \n\nAs for rain chances, Low pressure will move through the Upper  \nMidwest and into the Great Lakes Region by Thursday, with the \naccompanying surface front bringing a chance of rain showers to \nthe area Wednesday night and Thursday. Models have continued to \nback off on precipitation coverage, QPF trending lower. From \nwhat we are seeing, the best chance for showers will be along \nand west of the Blue Ridge (amounts 0.25 to 0.50), moisture \nalong the front thinning as it crosses the mountains and \nleaving areas east of the Blue Ridge with less than a quarter of \nan inch of wetting. Overall rainfall amounts Wed afternoon-\nFriday will average a third of an inch to three-quarters of an\ninch, with highest amounts in the Piedmont which need it due to\nthe drought.\n\nMay see some lingering upslope showers along the western slopes \nthrough Saturday given persistent NW flow. Otherwise, quiet \nweather once again returns through the remainder of the weekend.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Weekend Reset: Cooler, seasonable air returns \nthis weekend.\n\nThursday's frontal passage will begin transition to lower \ntemperatures for the weekend, readings closer to normal for  \nSaturday and Sunday. \n\nModels are advertising a clipper-like system to barrel through \nthe region late Sunday or Monday...this feature originating \nfrom the northern stream and cooler northern latitudes. \nDepending on its track it may draw down temperatures even \nfurther for Monday and Tuesday, suggesting a few days of cooler\nthan normal weather, a taste of what November should be like...\ncool and crisp.",
